DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       iippnnaatt  opens  the filename given (treating "-" as stdin) and parses the
       file for a set of rules which are to be added or removed  from  the  IP
       NAT.

       Each  rule processed by iippnnaatt is added to the kernels internal lists if
       there are no parsing problems.  Rules are added to the end of  the  in‐
       ternal lists, matching the order in which they appear when given to iipp‐‐
       nnaatt.

       Note that if iippff((88)) is not enabled when NAT is configured, it  will  be
       enabled  automatically,  as the same kernel facilities are used for NAT
       functionality.  In addition, packet forwarding must be enabled.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --CC     delete all entries in the current NAT rule listing (NAT rules)

       --dd     Enable printing of some extra debugging information.

       --FF     delete all active entries in the current NAT  translation  table
              (currently active NAT mappings)

       --hh     Print number of hits for each MAP/Redirect filter.

       --ll     Show the list of current NAT table entry mappings.

       --nn     This  flag  (no-change)  prevents  iippff  from actually making any
              ioctl calls or doing anything which would  alter  the  currently
              running kernel.

       --pp     This  flag is used with the --rr flag to cause any active NAT ses‐
              sions that were created by the rules being removed and that  are
              currently active to also be removed.

       --rr     Remove  matching  NAT rules rather than add them to the internal
              lists.

       --ss     Retrieve and display NAT statistics.

       --vv     Turn verbose mode on.  Displays  information  relating  to  rule
              processing and active rules/table entries.

